{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RS6aXWyEZpDqYorgs8GGUC","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent! Now I have all the information I need. Let me compile my findings:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've completed a thorough search through the go-unix-utils repository to find your requested information. Here are my key findings:\n\n### 1. ExpectedFiles Usage in Differential Tests\n\nWhile `ExpectedFiles` is defined in the testutils package, **there are currently NO test files in cmd/ that actively use it**. The field exists in `DiffTest` struct but hasn't been deployed yet in active tests.\n\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5132/pkg/testutils/difftest.go`\n\nThe `checkExpectedFiles()` function (lines 211-227) shows how it works:\n- Takes a map of relative paths to expected byte content\n- Reads each file from WorkDir\n- Compares actual content to expected content\n- Fails the test if files are missing or diverge\n\n### 2. File-Writing Test Pattern (sponge example)\n\nThe most comprehensive example of file-output differential testing comes from an earlier commit that had a sponge test file. While the current HEAD doesn't have a sponge test file, git history shows how file-writing utilities handle the sequential execution concern:\n\n**Pattern from historical sponge_test.go (commit c1721572):**\n\nInstead of using `ExpectedFiles` in `RunDiffTests`, the sponge tests use **separate WorkDirs**:\n- They create `refDir := t.TempDir()` and `goDir := t.TempDir()`\n- Run the reference binary against `refDir/out.txt`\n- Run the go binary against `goDir/out.txt`\n- Both binaries write to different files, avoiding conflicts\n- Then manually read and compare the output files\n\nThis pattern solves the \"both binaries run sequentially in same WorkDir\" problem by **isolating them to different directories**.\n\n### 3. Reference Binary Naming Convention\n\nThe pattern is clear and consistent - **GNU coreutils names prefixed with 'g'**:\n- `gcat` - GNU cat\n- `gtrue` - GNU true  \n- `gfalse` - GNU false\n\n**Key observation:** For moreutils binaries (like sponge), the historical test used plain `LookPath(\"sponge\")` (not `gsponge`), indicating moreutils doesn't follow the 'g' prefix convention.\n\n---\n\n## Representative Test Files\n\n### File 1: Current cat_test.go (WITH RunDiffTests)\n**Path:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5132/cmd/cat/cat_test.go`\n\nThis shows:\n- Line 21: `exec.LookPath(\"gcat\")` - reference binary lookup pattern\n- Line 55: `WorkDir: dir` - how tests set up isolated directories\n- Line 57: `testutils.RunDiffTests()` - standard pattern for stdout/stderr comparison\n- TestDiffFiles demonstrates file argument handling with WorkDir isolation\n\n### File 2: Historical sponge_test.go (File-output example)\n**Git commit:** `c1721572`\n\nThis comprehensive test shows:\n- `compareFileOutput()` function (lines 95-120): The pattern for file-writing utilities\n  - Creates separate refDir and goDir (line 98-99)\n  - Runs reference binary on refDir path (line 102)\n  - Runs go binary on goDir path (line 103)\n  - Reads back and compares actual file content (lines 105-115)\n- `testAppendMode()` function: Demonstrates handling of flags with file I/O\n- `testSymlinkOutput()` function: Shows how to test special file types while keeping binaries isolated\n\n---\n\n## Key Insights for Your Use Case\n\n1. **ExpectedFiles is ready to use but not yet deployed** - it's defined in DiffTest struct with a checker function that works correctly\n\n2. **Sequential execution in same WorkDir is already handled** - tests create separate WorkDirs for go and ref binaries to avoid file conflicts\n\n3. **For file-writing tests, the recommended pattern is:**\n   - Create separate temp directories for each binary\n   - Run binaries against isolated output paths\n   - Read and compare file contents after execution\n   - This avoids any sequencing issues entirely\n\n4. **Reference binary convention:** Use 'g' prefix for coreutils (gcat, gtrue, gfalse) but plain names for moreutils (sponge, not gsponge)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c6419ad2-62a1-4d44-aa74-38d7cef3c643","uuid":"90047b9c-c4a9-4448-8156-85053c9175c8","timestamp":"2026-05-14T03:05:00.663Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5132, I need to find:\n\n1. An existing cmd/*_test.go file that uses testutils.RunDiffTests with ExpectedFiles (file-writing tests, not just stdout comparison). Look through several cmd/ test files.\n2. Any existing cmd/ test that deals with file-writing utilities (like tee, sponge, etc.) to see how they handle the fact that both binaries run in the same WorkDir sequentially.\n3. The reference binary name convention - check if any test uses \"sponge\" or \"gsponge\" as a LookPath target. Also check if there's a pattern for moreutils binaries vs coreutils binaries.\n\nSearch broadly across cmd/*/\\*_test.go files. Report the full content of 1-2 representative test files that use ExpectedFiles or deal with file output.","agentId":"ab4c0da91fce0cf3f","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent! Now I have all the information I need. Let me compile my findings:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've completed a thorough search through the go-unix-utils repository to find your requested information. Here are my key findings:\n\n### 1. ExpectedFiles Usage in Differential Tests\n\nWhile `ExpectedFiles` is defined in the testutils package, **there are currently NO test files in cmd/ that actively use it**. The field exists in `DiffTest` struct but hasn't been deployed yet in active tests.\n\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5132/pkg/testutils/difftest.go`\n\nThe `checkExpectedFiles()` function (lines 211-227) shows how it works:\n- Takes a map of relative paths to expected byte content\n- Reads each file from WorkDir\n- Compares actual content to expected content\n- Fails the test if files are missing or diverge\n\n### 2. File-Writing Test Pattern (sponge example)\n\nThe most comprehensive example of file-output differential testing comes from an earlier commit that had a sponge test file. While the current HEAD doesn't have a sponge test file, git history shows how file-writing utilities handle the sequential execution concern:\n\n**Pattern from historical sponge_test.go (commit c1721572):**\n\nInstead of using `ExpectedFiles` in `RunDiffTests`, the sponge tests use **separate WorkDirs**:\n- They create `refDir := t.TempDir()` and `goDir := t.TempDir()`\n- Run the reference binary against `refDir/out.txt`\n- Run the go binary against `goDir/out.txt`\n- Both binaries write to different files, avoiding conflicts\n- Then manually read and compare the output files\n\nThis pattern solves the \"both binaries run sequentially in same WorkDir\" problem by **isolating them to different directories**.\n\n### 3. Reference Binary Naming Convention\n\nThe pattern is clear and consistent - **GNU coreutils names prefixed with 'g'**:\n- `gcat` - GNU cat\n- `gtrue` - GNU true  \n- `gfalse` - GNU false\n\n**Key observation:** For moreutils binaries (like sponge), the historical test used plain `LookPath(\"sponge\")` (not `gsponge`), indicating moreutils doesn't follow the 'g' prefix convention.\n\n---\n\n## Representative Test Files\n\n### File 1: Current cat_test.go (WITH RunDiffTests)\n**Path:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5132/cmd/cat/cat_test.go`\n\nThis shows:\n- Line 21: `exec.LookPath(\"gcat\")` - reference binary lookup pattern\n- Line 55: `WorkDir: dir` - how tests set up isolated directories\n- Line 57: `testutils.RunDiffTests()` - standard pattern for stdout/stderr comparison\n- TestDiffFiles demonstrates file argument handling with WorkDir isolation\n\n### File 2: Historical sponge_test.go (File-output example)\n**Git commit:** `c1721572`\n\nThis comprehensive test shows:\n- `compareFileOutput()` function (lines 95-120): The pattern for file-writing utilities\n  - Creates separate refDir and goDir (line 98-99)\n  - Runs reference binary on refDir path (line 102)\n  - Runs go binary on goDir path (line 103)\n  - Reads back and compares actual file content (lines 105-115)\n- `testAppendMode()` function: Demonstrates handling of flags with file I/O\n- `testSymlinkOutput()` function: Shows how to test special file types while keeping binaries isolated\n\n---\n\n## Key Insights for Your Use Case\n\n1. **ExpectedFiles is ready to use but not yet deployed** - it's defined in DiffTest struct with a checker function that works correctly\n\n2. **Sequential execution in same WorkDir is already handled** - tests create separate WorkDirs for go and ref binaries to avoid file conflicts\n\n3. **For file-writing tests, the recommended pattern is:**\n   - Create separate temp directories for each binary\n   - Run binaries against isolated output paths\n   - Read and compare file contents after execution\n   - This avoids any sequencing issues entirely\n\n4. **Reference binary convention:** Use 'g' prefix for coreutils (gcat, gtrue, gfalse) but plain names for moreutils (sponge, not gsponge)"}],"totalDurationMs":67013,"totalTokens":49164,"totalToolUseCount":33,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":502,"cache_read_input_tokens":47562,"output_tokens":1100,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":502},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1100,"cache_read_input_tokens":47562,"cache_creation_input_tokens":502,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":502,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"stand